Hi Helen, following on from above, I've had a look at the prod geo databases (bst-iaz-sql-prd-geo, ~£11.4k/month). Prod is genuinely busy, so it's a different picture from PPE, but there is still a saving:

GeoCuratedData (Serverless, max 32 / min 16): ~£7.9k/month
• CPU peaked at 60–95% in July and August, so max 32 is justified and shouldn't change.
• However, App CPU billed shows it's been charged at its 16 vCore minimum almost continuously (e.g. 43.45M vCore-seconds for 16 Aug–16 Sep, against 42.85M for a constant 16). Average CPU use is well under 1 vCore; the peaks are short and handled by scaling.
• The minimum appears to have been raised from ~4 to 16 around 24 July, at the same time as the max was increased (which was needed, as CPU was hitting 90–100% beforehand).
• Lowering the min to around 10, with max staying at 32, would save up to ~£2.9k/month (~£35k/year) with no change to peak capacity. Had it been 10 since July, that would have been around £7.5k so far. The trade-off is a slightly smaller minimum cache, so query performance should be monitored afterwards.

GeoLocators (Premium P2)
• Idle in prod (0% CPU/IO over 30 days), so moving to P1 would roughly halve its cost while staying on Premium.